  "stages": [
    {
      "id": "SF0",
      "title": "The ledger closes - the core run\u0027s leftovers",
      "sessions": 3,
      "notes": "SPEC: docs/history/CONDUCTOR-SARBAN.md section SF0. The core run left two ledgers and no stage that will ever open again to clear them: eleven bugs it filed against itself (ledger ids 2,3,4,5,6,8,9,10,11,12,13) and the still-open rows in .conductor/followups.md. The bug ledger is RUN-scoped - bug list under this plan answers no run found - so those eleven are invisible to you in run.db; they are transcribed into followups.md under Carried forward from the core run\u0027s bug ledger, and that transcription is your source. SF0.1 kills the keys nothing reads and the lines nobody can trust - inert model pins, inert verifyEachDelivery, a TelegramService start line for a service that early-returned, and a run that never says its notification path is dead. SF0.2 makes the verdict count every claim and name the session it queues, including the only outright hang on the list and the harness git helper that made every NewCommits assertion vacuous. SF0.3 makes pids and background work honest and finally delivers FU-OWNER-9\u0027s self-PID guard, which matters more now that a second run shares this machine. SF0.4 stops open bugs from vanishing with their run and reconciles every remaining followup row - fixed, closed with evidence, or re-homed to an owner that can act; never deleted.",
      "ownerGate": false,
      "kind": "deliver"
    },
    {
      "id": "SF1",
      "title": "The face sheds dead weight",
      "sessions": 2,
      "notes": "SPEC: docs/history/CONDUCTOR-SARBAN.md section SF1. Verifier scores get a real DTO first, then the Dev SQL console goes with all its traces - tab, endpoint, report query flag - while the MCP run_query stays for chat and the two non-SQL Dev panels are re-homed, not deleted. Then tabs consolidate to at most ten with a design note before code. The face agent map in the spec names every reference site.",
      "ownerGate": false,
      "kind": "deliver"
    },
    {
      "id": "SF2",
      "title": "The face tells the truth kindly - state, time, money",
      "sessions": 3,
      "notes": "SPEC: docs/history/CONDUCTOR-SARBAN.md section SF2, including the screenshot-critique table. Home reorganized around what-is-happening-and-what-should-I-do with one honest connection line and a last-run summary when offline; one shared time formatter with relative age and the Timeline UTC mislabel fixed; money honesty - over-budget renders as OVER, window vs lifetime distinguished, in-flight session cost live in the top bar, and the attempts marker gets a legend.",
      "ownerGate": false,
      "kind": "deliver"
    },
    {
      "id": "SF3",
      "title": "Reading a session becomes cheap",
      "sessions": 3,
      "notes": "SPEC: docs/history/CONDUCTOR-SARBAN.md section SF3. The digest layer spends what SC7 captured: one-liner tool calls, a per-session digest panel with the bg-purpose storyline, rune-safe fold; the kanban answers where-are-we - stage grouping, active-stage highlight, card meta without selection, column totals, separated skips, in-column scroll, a you-are-here ribbon; git awareness lands on the wire and in the face - branch chip, dirty state, ahead-behind, commit subjects. SF3.3 also carries FU-OWNER-10 in .conductor/followups.md - GET /state names no engine version, commit or face build, so the face cannot show which build you are attached to and did-my-reinstall-take took four out-of-band checks to answer. Add engineVersion, engineCommit and faceBuild to the same payload SF3.3 already opens, and the short form to the status strip beside the branch chip.",
      "ownerGate": false,
      "kind": "deliver"
    },
    {
      "id": "SF4",
      "title": "The human queue is a first-class surface",
      "sessions": 2,
      "notes": "SPEC: docs/history/CONDUCTOR-SARBAN.md section SF4. Productize the SHAHIN.md pattern: the engine collects every open HUMAN line, ownerGated stage, park with reason and age, blocked-until wait and owner-marked item into OWNER-QUEUE.md and an owner-queue endpoint, each entry saying what it unblocks and the exact command that clears it; the face surfaces it with age, and a new item pushes to Telegram. SF4.2 owns the push path, so two owner rows in .conductor/followups.md land with it. FU-OWNER-11: a push carries no identity - no repo, plan, run id or build - so one chat receiving two machines cannot attribute a line and a message read hours later cannot be dated to a build; prefix every push with plan name and session number and carry repo plus engine version in the run-start and run-end messages, taking the version from FU-OWNER-10\u0027s field. FU-OWNER-13: between a saved plan edit and the next session boundary, POST /telegram/token and GET /telegram/status both answer add a telegram block to the plan - advising the edit that was just accepted - because both read the in-memory PlanConfig while the reload is queued; when a reload is pending both must say so instead and the status payload needs a reloadPending flag so the face can show waiting, not unconfigured. That is the SC1.3 failure one layer out.",
      "ownerGate": false,
      "kind": "deliver"
    },
    {
      "id": "SF5",
      "title": "Supervision without a polling meter",
      "sessions": 4,
      "notes": "SPEC: docs/history/CONDUCTOR-SARBAN.md section SF5. The watch verb blocks silently and returns only on the wake set - park, circuit breaker, budget park, phase RED twice on one stage, engine gone, run ended - never on backoffs or session churn, with a json brief of about thirty lines; a supervisor plan block runs a command on wake with the brief on stdin; the remote pattern is documented and proven once; conductor ps lists every run on the machine and the face gains a run picker. The watch-run skill (.claude/skills/watch-run/SKILL.md, in this repo) is the behavioural spec. Prove watch against a scratch run of YOUR build (promptExtra trap 2); conductor ps is read-only, so listing the run driving you is legitimate evidence.",
      "ownerGate": false,
      "kind": "deliver"
    },
    {
      "id": "SF6",
      "title": "The prompt bank compounds",
      "sessions": 2,
      "notes": "SPEC: docs/history/CONDUCTOR-SARBAN.md section SF6. Built-in session and fix templates carry the field lessons - mark in-progress first, claim before the handoff, the deferred-MCP note with the CLI fallback, long commands under conductor bg, the anchor-commit rule for multi-repo plans; the bank under plans/ is pruned, enriched from the rounds and indexed; conductor init scaffolds the refreshed set and passes doctor clean.",
      "ownerGate": false,
      "kind": "deliver"
    },
    {
      "id": "SF7",
      "title": "Ship the era",
      "sessions": 2,
      "notes": "SPEC: docs/history/CONDUCTOR-SARBAN.md section SF7. Docs reconciled with reality - plan-config advisor default, tracker runtime files, operating gains the supervision section, NEXT-FEATURES refreshed, the field notes gain a closure ledger; era CHANGELOG; then the owner-signed merge of feat/sarban to master, the tagged release through the SC8 pipeline, and a reinstall whose conductor version matches the releases page. The closure ledger covers both era ledgers, not just the field notes: every followups.md row and every core-run bug id says which stage closed it and with which commit, and anything still open names a living owner. The reinstall in SF7.2 is the FIRST install.ps1 of this run and only after the owner confirms no other conductor run is live on the machine.",
      "ownerGate": true,
      "kind": "deliver"
    }
