  "promptExtra": "TRAPS THIS REPO HAS ALREADY PAID FOR, in order of cost:\n0. A SECOND CONDUCTOR RUN IS LIVE ON THIS MACHINE, in another repo, and you can break it without touching it. Never run tools/install.ps1 - both runs execute the same published binaries. Never kill a conductor.exe, conductor-face.exe or stray dotnet process by pid: check its command line with Get-CimInstance Win32_Process and leave anything whose path is not this repo alone, no matter how stale it looks. Trap 6\u0027s permission to stop stray dotnet hosts was written for a machine running one thing. Give every scratch rig its own port and its own state dir - the other run holds 4317 - and read the port back from the rig\u0027s own discovery file instead of assuming a default. Usage-limit backoffs will be more frequent because both runs draw on one subscription; they self-resume and are not your problem.\n1. NEVER run tools/install.ps1 or overwrite the published engine - the conductor driving you runs from that published copy, and so does the other run. The owner reinstalls at SF7.2, not before.\n2. This repo\u0027s .conductor/ is the LIVE state of the run driving you. The claim and note verbs (conductor task, note, bug, bg) and read-only verbs (status, task --list) are yours; NEVER aim run-control verbs (run, pause, resume, abort, approve, plan set, plan reload, goto, skip, retry-stage, rollback, kill) at this repo. Any live-run proof spawns YOUR build against a scratch repo with its own tiny plan and its own .conductor - for example under %TEMP%\\sarban-proofs - never against C:/code/conductor.\n3. The conductor on PATH is the published engine driving you - post-core, but still NOT your working tree. Exercise YOUR changes through the fresh build: dotnet run --project src/Conductor -- VERB (or go run ./cmd/... in face-go). Testing a new verb through the PATH shim only proves the published engine lacks it. Exception by design: task claims go through the PATH copy, because they target the driving run.\n4. The ratchet gate is tools/gates/ratchet.ps1 - the wrong path prints an error but exits 0, a silent false green. Never raise the pragma ceiling; fix the cause.\n5. face-go: pad plain text then style, never width-format an ANSI string; clamp with MaxWidth and MaxHeight; goldens pin UTC and live in face-go/internal/tui/testdata/golden - regenerate them in a separate rebaseline commit, and read face-go/STYLE.md before any face change.\n6. Orphaned dotnet test hosts flake the full suite. Stopping one is sanctioned ONLY after trap 0\u0027s command-line check proves it belongs to this repo; never kill a conductor process, yours or anyone\u0027s.\n7. PowerShell tooling targets Windows PowerShell 5.1 and must stay ASCII-only - em-dashes have torn string literals here before.\n8. Keep tracker handoffs, checkpoint titles and all plan prose free of curly braces until you have verified the SC3.3 engine fix is what is driving you.\n9. The conductor MCP task tools may arrive deferred in your harness - ToolSearch for task_update first, or use the CLI: conductor task --done ID --evidence PATH. That command is the only claim; prose moves nothing.\n10. Commit per checkpoint with the repo\u0027s trailer convention and push feat/sarban. Go sources are checked out LF; keep them that way.\n11. Tab changes: tabKey in model.go is the single source for mnemonics; the help legend in cmdbar.go is hand-maintained - update both or the help lies.\n12. The core run\u0027s eleven open bugs are NOT in your run.db - the bug ledger is run-scoped and this plan starts a new run. Read them from .conductor/followups.md, section Carried forward from the core run\u0027s bug ledger, and file anything new with conductor bug new so SF0.4 can close the loop.",
